INT. FUNERAL RECEPTION - COMMUNITY HALL - DAY

Trays of sandwiches, a slideshow looping on a projector. Coach Frank Dunleavy's face, forty years of whistles and clipboards, fills the wall.

Two teenagers in oversized suits fight over the last deviled egg while a waiter weaves past them with a tray of lukewarm coffee and a little brown dog sneaks under the buffet table.

Ray Pruitt, 50s, former star pitcher, stands by the window with a paper cup. Across the room Luis Navarro, 50s, his old catcher, enters, spots him, and stops dead.

INSERT - THE SLIDESHOW

A photo of both men, young, arms around each other, a championship trophy between them.

BACK TO SCENE

Luis crosses the room slowly. Ray doesn't turn.

LUIS
You came.

RAY
He was my coach too.

LUIS
He was his coach more than he was yours. After what you did to him he barley spoke your name.

RAY
(finally turning)
What I did to him. Okay.

CLOSE ON Ray's hand tightening around the cup, coffee spilling over his knuckles.

LUIS
You threw that game. Everybody knew. Frank knew. he took it to his grave and so did I untill today.

RAY
You want to do this at his funeral.

LUIS
You wanted to do it at his funeral. You wore the ring.

A teenager drops a plate. The waiter freezes. The dog yips. The whole room turns, and the slideshow flips to the next photo, Frank holding a trophy over his head.

RAY
(very quietly)
I'm sorry.

LUIS
(blinking, his anger leaving all at once)
Say it to him.

Ray sets the cup on the sill, walks to the projector, and stands in its beam so Frank's smile falls across his chest.
